      "text": "To determine the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we start by simplifying each term in the list.\n\nEach term in the list is of the form \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) for \\(n = 1, 2, \\ldots, 1000\\). We need to find the range of values that each term can take. The minimum value occurs when \\(n = 1\\):\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0.\n\\]\nThe maximum value occurs when \\(n = 1000\\):\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 1000 \\right\\rfloor = 1000.\n\\]\nThus, the terms range from 0 to 1000, inclusive. We need to check if all integers from 0 to 1000 are indeed in the list. This is true because for any \\(n\\) in the range from 1 to 1000, \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) will be an integer plus possibly a fraction less than 1, and the floor function will simply take the integer part.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct numbers in the list is the number of integers from 0 to 1000, which is:\n\\[\n1000 - 0 + 1 = 1001.\n\\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1001}.\n\\]",
