      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we first need to understand the nature of the floor function and the sequence.\n\nThe general term of the sequence is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\). We need to determine the range of values that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) can take as \\(n\\) varies from 1 to 1000.\n\nFirst, let's find the smallest value of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\):\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{4}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 0.004 \\right\\rfloor = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{9}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 0.009 \\right\\rfloor = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n\\vdots\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000000}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 1000 \\right\\rfloor = 1000\n\\]\n\nThe sequence starts at 0 and goes up to 1000. We need to determine if there are any other distinct values in this sequence. To do this, we need to find the smallest \\(n\\) such that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\geq 1\\):\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = k \\implies k \\leq \\frac{n^2}{1000} < k+1 \\implies n^2 \\leq 1000(k+1) < (k+1)^2\n\\]\n\\[\nn^2 \\leq 1000(k+1) \\implies n \\leq \\sqrt{1000(k+1)}\n\\]\n\\[\nn^2 > 1000k \\implies n > \\sqrt{1000k}\n\\]\nSo, \\(n\\) must satisfy:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1000k} < n \\leq \\sqrt{1000(k+1)}\n\\]\nWe need to find the maximum \\(k\\) such that \\(\\sqrt{1000(k+1)} - \\sqrt{1000k} < 1000\\). Let's approximate this:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1000(k+1)} - \\sqrt{1000k} < 1000 \\implies \\sqrt{1000k(1 + \\frac{1}{k})} - \\sqrt{1000k} < 1000 \\implies \\sqrt{1000(k+1)} - \\sqrt{1000k} < 1000\\sqrt{k}\n\\]\nFor large \\(k\\), \\(\\sqrt{k+1} \\approx \\sqrt{k} + \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{k}}\\), so:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1000(k+1)} \\approx \\sqrt{1000k} + 50\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1000k} + 50 - \\sqrt{1000k} < 1000\\sqrt{k} \\implies 50 < 1000\\sqrt{k}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt{k} > \\frac{1}{20} \\implies k > \\frac{1}{400}\n\\]\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, the maximum value of \\(k\\) is 149. Therefore, the distinct values are from \\(k=0\\) to \\(k=149\\), inclusive, which gives us a total of 150 distinct values.\n\nThus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is \\(\\boxed{150}\\).",
